# Port of tests/process_sessions.rs:314-371; source verbatim after original format expansion,
# except for a 5s stage timeout: under host load a nice'd tree and descendant can take over a
# second to start, and 100ms (or 1s) could fire before the descendant had published its pid.
# ORACLE-LITERAL; PENDING-SAME-HEAD; original cfg(unix); have_rustc guard erased.
# Each tier's descendants are judged right after the step that spawned them, by
# the fixture's `reaped` mode: gone, a zombie, exiting (PF_EXITING), or holding a
# pending SIGKILL, within 2s. A killed descendant can linger in state D (wchan
# rq_qos_wait, block-I/O throttling) for seconds under host I/O load before its
# /proc entry goes; liveness alone would call that a survivor. `reaped` removes
# each pid file, so the next step's descendants write fresh ones.
opt-in: timer-bound (about 30 s): three tiers each wait out a 5 s stage timeout and a 2 s reap window; run with --opt-in, the registries profile or its exact id
requires: os:unix
requires: tool:cat
requires: tool:kill
fixture: subjects
step: jet build native/session_fixture.jet
exit: 0
copy: native/.jet/build/session_fixture session_fixture
write: run.jet process_pipeline_timeout_reaps_descendants_across_execution_tiers.source
step default: jet run run.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = process_pipeline_timeout_reaps_descendants_across_execution_tiers.out
step: {work}/session_fixture reaped {work}/aot.pid {work}/jit.pid {work}/interp.pid
exit: 0
step: jet build run.jet
exit: 0
step: built
exit: == default
stdout: == default
stderr: == default
step: {work}/session_fixture reaped {work}/aot.pid {work}/jit.pid {work}/interp.pid
exit: 0
